analyses.2:
All three round-1 trials trained without errors, but the scores are hidden, so I can't rank them or claim any of them beat the baseline. With no score signal I'm choosing on two grounds: what the task needs and spreading the bets. The final selection uses real dev scores from the 3 best trials, so I want several strong candidates that differ from each other. Each footprint-matched placement logit needs to know whether every cell under the brick is missing at its landing layer and whether those cells are level. A deeper body or a body that sees the whole grid should help the network learn which layer a brick lands on, and should help decide placement order and DONE. This round:
(1) combines two round-1 ideas: the footprint head on the deeper body;
(2) tests a residual, normalised body of depth 4 with coordinate channels, whose 9x9 receptive field covers the whole 8x8 grid;
(3) tests a new idea, a global self-attention block, for reasoning about order across the whole grid and for deciding DONE.
All three stay under about 1.7x the starting compute.

analyses.4:
No scores are visible, so the only evidence is that all 9 trials trained without errors. Every candidate needed between 1.05x and 1.73x the starting compute. Large compute increases cost training steps, so I am keeping all three final candidates at 1.4x or below. Two ideas are repeated in some form in this round. The first is the footprint-shaped placement head, because it matches the task: a brick is right only if every cell it covers is right. The second is whole-grid context, because placement order depends on what is still missing elsewhere. This is the last round, and these trials only matter if they reach the top 3. So each candidate combines one plausible strong idea with one new cheap ingredient that the search has not tested yet:
- the legality mask as an input feature;
- a global feature vector fed back into every cell's placement score;
- a dilated conv layer for whole-grid reach at almost no extra compute.
